                <head>334. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>British Prime Minister Macmillan visited
                    Washington March 19–24, 1959, at President Eisenhower’s
                    invitation for informal discussions on the international situation. Foreign
                    Secretary Lloyd accompanied him. The
                    subject of Libya arose briefly during a March 22 conversation on the Middle
                    East: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Mr. Lloyd observed that Libya
                            could be a stable area if Nasser
                            were not intriguing there. The Prime Minister said that applied to the
                            Sudan as well. The President inquired why we could not get some teachers
                            into Libya where the Egyptians already had so many.” (Memorandum of
                            conversation, USDel/MC/26; Eisenhower Library, Whitman File,
                            International File)</p>
                    </quote></p>
            </div>
